// @openui "The Psychology of Motivation" "Why people actually do things"
root = Stack(
  [
    TextContent("**Intrinsic motivation** is doing an activity because it is inherently interesting, satisfying, or enjoyable — the fuel behind long-term engagement. People keep learning, playing, or building not because something is promised to them, but because the activity itself is rewarding."),
    TextContent("**Extrinsic motivation** is doing an activity for separable outcomes — rewards, grades, money, or to avoid punishment. It is powerful in the short term, which is why deadlines and bonuses work, but it is fragile long-term: the moment the outcome stops being delivered, the behavior often stops too."),
    TextContent("**Self-Determination Theory** (Deci & Ryan) proposes that three basic psychological needs sustain intrinsic motivation: **autonomy** (choice and ownership over what you do), **competence** (feeling effective and growing at something), and **relatedness** (connection to other people). Systems that satisfy these needs keep people engaged; systems that violate them quietly switch people off."),
    TextContent("The **overjustification effect** is the classic warning: when an external reward is attached to something people already enjoy, they start attributing their behavior to the reward instead of the activity — and intrinsic motivation drops. In the famous studies, paying children to draw made them draw less, not more, once the payments stopped."),
    TextContent("This is why naive \"sprinkles and donuts\" gamification — slapping points, badges, and leaderboards onto anything — often decays within weeks. The external trinkets crowd out the motivation the activity already had, and when the novelty of the trinkets wears off, so does engagement."),
    TextContent("**Variable rewards** (unpredictable outcomes) are the strongest engagement hook — the same variable-ratio reinforcement that makes slot machines compelling. Loot boxes, surprise XP bonuses, and randomized streak celebrations all exploit this: you never know what the next action will yield, so you keep acting."),
    TextContent("**Loss aversion** is the finding that people feel the pain of losing roughly twice as strongly as the pleasure of gaining an equivalent amount. Streaks convert your progress into something you can lose, which is exactly why they work — and exactly why they can burn users out the moment a streak snaps."),
    TextContent("**Flow** (Csikszentmihalyi) is the state of deep engagement that happens when challenge matches skill: hard enough to demand attention, easy enough to stay in control. Games keep players in flow by dynamically tuning difficulty; gamified systems should do the same rather than piling on static points that neither stretch nor support the learner."),
    TextContent("The design implication is to start from **motivation** — which need or drive the user has — and then pick **mechanics** that express it. Points and badges are merely tools; they are meaningless unless they are connected to genuine progress, meaning, or social connection."),
    TextContent("A practical rule of thumb: ask \"is the user here because the activity itself is rewarding, or because we told them it would be?\" If the honest answer is the second one, your system will break the day the novelty wears off."),
    TextContent("An ethical note: motivation mechanics can also be **dark patterns** — manufactured scarcity, guilt-tripping notifications, and streak anxiety manipulate rather than motivate. Sustainable gamification respects autonomy, explains why a mechanic exists, and offers real opt-outs."),
  ],
)
